---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Kubernetes LLM Inference Platform"
  description="A GitOps-managed platform for self-hosted LLM inference: GPU substrate, vLLM/KServe, inference-aware routing, LiteLLM budgets, observability, and developer AI workflows."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Project</p>
  <h1>Kubernetes LLM Inference Platform</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    A GitOps-managed platform for self-hosted LLM inference: GPU substrate, vLLM/KServe,
    inference-aware routing, tenant budgets, observability, and developer AI workflows.
  </p>

  <div class="project-actions">
    <a class="linked" href="/blog/missing-control-plane-above-vllm">Read the flagship essay</a>
    <a class="linked" href="/blog/gpu-100-percent-is-lying">Read the benchmark story</a>
  </div>

  <hr />

  <section class="project-hero">
    <p>
      Self-hosting a model is easy to demo and hard to operate. The missing layer is everything
      around the model server: who can call it, which model they can see, how much they can spend,
      where the request should route, which signal should scale capacity, how secrets enter the
      cluster, and how you know the system is healthy.
    </p>
    <p>
      This project turns those decisions into a runnable Kubernetes stack. It is not a chatbot app.
      The model server is one workload inside a platform.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-label">Proof stack</h2>
    <div class="proof-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Core proof</h3>
        <p>
          GPU Kubernetes on GKE, raw vLLM serving, Prometheus/Grafana/DCGM telemetry, and benchmark
          data for TTFT, ITL, throughput, queue depth, and KV-cache pressure.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Portability proof</h3>
        <p>
          OpenTofu owns the substrate; Argo CD owns the in-cluster platform. GKE consumes managed
          GPU plumbing; Hetzner validates the self-managed GPU Operator path.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Platform proof</h3>
        <p>
          LiteLLM virtual keys and budgets, GIE inference-aware routing, KEDA queue-depth autoscale,
          External Secrets, and KServe/llm-d as documented graduation paths.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Workflow proof</h3>
        <p>
          Open WebUI, Tabby, Coder, and agent tooling sit above the inference platform instead of
          turning the repo into an AI homelab bundle.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-label">Request path</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The request path is layered on purpose:</p>
      <pre><code>Client / developer tool
  -> LiteLLM tenant edge
  -> Gateway API + GIE routing
  -> vLLM / KServe serving layer
  -> GPU substrate</code></pre>
      <p>
        The important decision is not the component list. It is the ownership boundary: LiteLLM owns
        tenant economics, GIE owns endpoint selection, vLLM owns token serving, KEDA owns
        queue-depth autoscaling, OpenTofu owns the cloud substrate, Argo CD owns the in-cluster
        platform, and External Secrets owns runtime secret delivery.
      </p>
      <p>
        <img
          src="/assets/llmaas-two-layers.svg"
          alt="LiteLLM owns economics while GIE owns inference-aware routing."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-label">Selected results</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>On one NVIDIA L4 serving <code>Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct</code> with vLLM:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Signal</th>
            <th>Observed result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>GPU utilization</td>
            <td>100%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>KV-cache usage</td>
            <td>1.1%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Waiting requests</td>
            <td>0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Throughput at concurrency 32</td>
            <td>3,773 output tok/s</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>TTFT p50, concurrency 1 to 32</td>
            <td>27 ms to 135 ms</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The lesson: GPU utilization alone was the wrong scaling signal. Queue depth carried the
        useful backpressure signal.
      </p>
      <p>
        <img
          src="/assets/vllm-serving-metrics.png"
          alt="vLLM serving dashboard with latency, throughput, queue depth, KV cache, and GPU metrics."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-label">What this is not</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a claim that a single-GPU lab is a finished production SaaS. The lab validates the
      shape of the platform cheaply. HA, hard multi-tenancy, alerting, and larger-scale operations
      have explicit adoption triggers rather than being implied by the demo.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-label">Related writing</h2>
    <ul class="post-list">
      <li>
        <a class="title" href="/blog/missing-control-plane-above-vllm">The Missing Control Plane Above vLLM</a>
        <span class="date">flagship</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="title" href="/blog/gpu-100-percent-is-lying">Your GPU says 100%. It's lying to you.</a>
        <span class="date">benchmarks</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="title" href="/blog/gpu-cloud-agnostic-breaks">The GPU layer is where cloud-agnostic quietly breaks</a>
        <span class="date">substrate</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
